  h_ml  like-for-like      frozen@75   FREEZING costs  ANCHORING gives
               target         target    (sigma_Delta)      (log-units)
    50         252.12         244.71            -0.37             80.9
    75         244.71         244.71             0.00             76.2
   100         234.98         244.71             0.49             70.3  <- pump
   140         224.53         244.71             1.02             64.2
   184         212.79         244.71             1.61             57.6  <- FITTED
   220         203.66         244.71             2.07             52.8

FREEZING AT h_ml = 75 overstates the miss by 1.61 sigma_Delta at the
FITTED h_ml = 184, because the model's ML box then spans 0-184 m while the
target is built on 0-75 m water. That bias is IN FAVOUR of adopting a pump
and of a stronger one -- i.e. it points at the conclusion the term exists
to test.
MODEL-ANCHORING gives a penalty falling 81 -> 53 log-units across
h_ml 50 -> 220, i.e. it REWARDS A LARGE h_ml and pushes AGAINST clause 1.

THE ONE COMBINATION THAT IS NEUTRAL ON BOTH COUNTS: freeze the layers, but
freeze them at the h_ml the refit actually lands on. Frozen removes the
feedback; matched removes the representation bias. It costs one iteration
and a gate on |h_ml_posterior - h_ml_target|.

AND A COINCIDENCE WORTH USING: at h_ml = 100 -- the value the pump
delivers (15d) -- the like-for-like target is +234.98 against the SHIPPED
rds's +233.11, a mismatch of 0.09 sigma_Delta. So IF the pump lands h_ml
near 100, the shipped fixed-depth layers ARE the like-for-like choice, and
the freeze-vs-anchor question dissolves. If it lands elsewhere, it does not.


=== PART 3b. PAIRED-COLUMN CHECK ON THE CROSS-LAYER SIGN ===
field             columns   corr(S,D)   bootstrap      agree?
DIC (TCO2)            390      -0.376      -0.602         YES
d13C                  349      +0.094      +0.124         YES
D14C (CONTROL)        460      -0.084      -0.001     both ~0

The two estimators AGREE on the sign for every field, so the DIC
anticorrelation is a property of the ocean and not of the block scheme.
MECHANISM: surface DIC is HIGHEST where the water is cold and dense --
the high latitudes -- and those are exactly the regions where the deep
water is YOUNGEST and so LOWEST in respired carbon. Basins therefore
trade the two ends of the gradient against each other, and a sample that
is basin-skewed misses the gradient by MORE than it misses either level.
This is the same young-Atlantic/old-Pacific contrast that drives the deep
D14C correlogram's negative tail, read on a different pair of layers.


